WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.200
McElligott: Let's look at the sustainable seafood case.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:07.303
And tell us what are the pieces
of that, and do they exist now?

00:00:07.303 --> 00:00:13.400
Are they out there and deployed?
Do we have IOT (Internet of Things) in the seas?

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:20.740
That's validating these things?
What's the ecosystem if you will of technology?

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:25.604
How much of those things are in place, and
how much is almost there or a bit further off?

00:00:25.604 --> 00:00:27.800
McElligott: Can you give us a sense? 
Behlendorf: Sure, sure.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.800
Behlendorf: I don't think it's a hardware problem.
Like I think there are devices now that are

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.660
available off-the-shelf to be able to do
the kinds of stuff I mentioned.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:39.940
There's a company called Smart Catch
that makes these net sensors.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:43.900
They even have video cameras on them. So you
can see the kind of fish that you caught.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:48.100
It's kind of crazy. There are sensors on buoys
and at ports and things like that

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.140
to collect that data. That's real.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:54.000
The blockchain side of that picture is really simple.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.620
It's, you know, you stand up a network of nodes...

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:00.140
I mean this is why Hyperledger was built.
This stuff is in production now.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:05.600
That ledger, that chain, is not in production yet. It's
still being developed. Intel is one of the leads on that.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:11.920
But they have gone to a number of the different fish processors and some of the major fleet operators.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:15.140
All of whom, what they're trying to prevent are the

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:19.800
renegade fishermen who actually
offload their catches to legit fishermen

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:26.240
in weird waters. Who then come back
into port and try to offload.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:32.140
I'd say probably the biggest challenge will be getting
a critical mass of the participants in any marketplace.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:36.040
At enough layers in the chain
from beginning to end

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:39.760
to start working together so that they basically shame...

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.500
by working well, shame the rest
into joining that network.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.560
And maybe it means eventually, if you're not part
of the network, you can't sell your fish.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:53.610
Not only to Whole Foods, but maybe not even
to any supply any retailer in the United States

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:57.479
So I think... there's a bootstrapping problem
with a lot of these things.

00:01:57.479 --> 00:02:02.640
McElligott: So to look at it a little bit more closely...

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:08.740
It's starting with the fishing level... to the merchant level is the end point. Along the way there are

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:16.040
transportation legs that are there. There may be the
port itself that needs to have some infrastructure there.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:23.180
And does it look like it would be something like
the "Good Housekeeping Seal" that this is like

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:29.920
certified... and again this isn't obviously your
piece of this, but I'm curious what you're hearing,

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:38.060
because you're talking to... in your role,
you're encountering every piece of the ecosystem

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:44.140
that are touching this stuff. Is it that
I would have an app and I would be able to see

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:50.220
that this fish... and verify it myself... or that the merchant is providing that certainty at that level?

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:53.740
Behlendorf: this is still predominantly
a business-to-business technology.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:58.620
And it should be the case that
they'll be eventually providers who act as

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.060
a kind of a portal for average consumers.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:05.020
So that in theory Whole Foods should be able
to provide you some sort of app that says

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:09.020
"Where did my fish come from?" that surface
the data back to that chain.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.920
You still have to trust that Whole Foods is telling you the truth, right?

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:17.040
But it could be at some point that
that ledger becomes fully public

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.900
and there's multiple ways to get to it and verify it.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:25.620
An example of an application I'd really like to see, that
looks more like that would be voter registration rolls.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:30.660
So a lot of people talk about, "Hey, should we use
the blockchain to help manage elections?"

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:34.980
And I think it's a  terrible idea to cast votes
using a blockchain.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:41.380
Because it's really hard to simultaneously preserve the
integrity of the election and the confidentiality of voters

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:43.000
while being able to audit it.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.120
But you could use a distributed ledger for the voter registration side of it.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:51.960
So that everyone's looking at the same database
with regards to who's registered to vote where.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:04:00.389
And you could use it at the tail end, too. Which is what's
the summary from each of the different polling places.

00:04:00.389 --> 00:04:06.480
And rolling that up to a state level
or national summary for how the vote went.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:11.140
And in both those cases having the
public be able to see that ledger, each

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:14.300
individual item, the entire spreadsheet so to speak,

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:17.240
is essential to building trust
and confidence in the process.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.800
So for that I would fight
for that to be very public.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:25.140
For something like diamonds, the participants in
that market have already gone through this complex,

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:30.640
political balancing act of sharing just enough
information that they can audit each other

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:32.560
on where these diamonds came from.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.080
But not sharing things like the prices they paid, right?

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:39.020
And other metadata that could be used by investors

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:42.780
perhaps to short stocks of companies that
they think might not be doing well.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:48.140
I'm a very pro "open data" kind of guy,
and transparency kind of guy.

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:51.420
But for a lot of good reasons,
the business communities

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:56.100
are only willing to share data if it means
they can't get access to other things.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:01.160
And in the case of the diamond industry there was
a mandate from the policymakers

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:05.040
to clean up their act when it came to
blood diamonds and that sort of thing.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.540
And that kind of pressure is essential
in a lot of these chains.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:13.000
I think these chains are a way to finally get things
that economists consider externalities in markets.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.780
finally reintegrated into the flow
of goods and commerce.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:23.740
Whether that's labor conditions
or carbon load, for example,

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:25.400
of the different products flowing through.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:29.280
Or other things correlating to
the sustainable development goals.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:32.480
So that's the idealist in me,
and that's why I'm involved in this.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:35.240
You've got to create just enough
economic value, business value,

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:36.780
for the businesses to pick it up and use it.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.600
Because they won't do that just endogenously.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:42.000
They need some business problem
that they can solve at the same time.

